1
00:00:03,102 --> 00:00:05,562
Imagine you run a busy shop.

2
00:00:05,813 --> 00:00:08,931
Your stock was promised for this morning.

3
00:00:09,436 --> 00:00:12,380
You told your own customers it would be on

4
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:14,075
the shelf by noon.

5
00:00:14,326 --> 00:00:17,600
Noon comes. The truck does not.

6
00:00:17,851 --> 00:00:21,421
Nobody calls. Nobody explains.

7
00:00:22,673 --> 00:00:27,266
At Halcyon Logistics, this happens to 29 of every 100

8
00:00:27,286 --> 00:00:28,471
deliveries.

9
00:00:30,322 --> 00:00:33,813
Halcyon delivers across the United States.

10
00:00:34,267 --> 00:00:39,589
In six months: 48,340 deliveries.

11
00:00:39,840 --> 00:00:43,369
Only 71 in 100 arrived on time.

12
00:00:43,823 --> 00:00:46,786
The industry benchmark is 94.

13
00:00:47,038 --> 00:00:51,464
Late deliveries cost about $1.74 million

14
00:00:51,484 --> 00:00:53,429
a year in penalties.

15
00:00:53,680 --> 00:00:57,616
The customer success team spends 64% of its

16
00:00:57,636 --> 00:01:01,703
week answering complaints. And in 12 of those

17
00:01:01,723 --> 00:01:04,744
complaints, the client says the same thing:

18
00:01:04,764 --> 00:01:06,386
"nobody told us."

19
00:01:06,638 --> 00:01:11,631
9 big clients, worth $14.2 million a year,

20
00:01:11,651 --> 00:01:13,635
are thinking of leaving.

21
00:01:13,886 --> 00:01:17,552
The plan on the table: buy new routing software,

22
00:01:17,572 --> 00:01:20,651
for $680,000.

23
00:01:20,902 --> 00:01:23,298
But is the route really the problem?

24
00:01:26,350 --> 00:01:28,166
This is DeliveryLens.

25
00:01:28,417 --> 00:01:31,806
DeliveryLens finds why deliveries are late.

26
00:01:32,285 --> 00:01:35,067
It fixes each cause, every day.

27
00:01:35,545 --> 00:01:39,268
And it proves the gain, with Halcyon's own data.

28
00:01:39,520 --> 00:01:43,580
Find why. Fix it. Prove it.

29
00:01:46,431 --> 00:01:49,710
DeliveryLens read every one of the 48,000

30
00:01:49,730 --> 00:01:50,902
deliveries.

31
00:01:51,153 --> 00:01:55,637
Five causes explain 28 of the 29 points lost.

32
00:01:55,888 --> 00:01:58,606
A weak carrier on certain lanes.

33
00:01:59,073 --> 00:02:03,073
An incomplete address. A same-day order that

34
00:02:03,093 --> 00:02:06,314
arrives after the 08:00 pick list.

35
00:02:06,779 --> 00:02:10,154
Stock that is not at the nearest warehouse.

36
00:02:10,593 --> 00:02:13,427
And a driver who leaves the planned route.

37
00:02:13,679 --> 00:02:16,900
Here is the same order, played twice.

38
00:02:17,353 --> 00:02:20,593
Today, the nearest warehouse is empty,

39
00:02:20,613 --> 00:02:23,222
so the truck crosses the country and arrives

40
00:02:23,242 --> 00:02:27,230
late. With DeliveryLens, a warehouse that

41
00:02:27,250 --> 00:02:31,115
has the stock ships it, and it arrives on time.

42
00:02:31,367 --> 00:02:34,943
And here is the surprise. The routing tool

43
00:02:34,963 --> 00:02:38,378
alone lifts on-time delivery to about 80.

44
00:02:38,829 --> 00:02:42,552
Fixing the five causes lifts it to about 90.

45
00:02:42,804 --> 00:02:45,787
You can test any mix of fixes yourself,

46
00:02:45,807 --> 00:02:47,700
in the what-if simulator.

47
00:02:49,552 --> 00:02:54,108
Now, the daily work. Orders arrive by email,

48
00:02:54,128 --> 00:02:58,966
in 14 different layouts. Staff spend 44 hours

49
00:02:58,986 --> 00:03:00,983
a week typing them in.

50
00:03:01,234 --> 00:03:03,495
On the DeliveryLens Order desk,

51
00:03:03,515 --> 00:03:06,781
an email goes in, and a checked warehouse

52
00:03:06,801 --> 00:03:08,321
order comes out.

53
00:03:08,572 --> 00:03:11,600
A safety screen checks the email first.

54
00:03:12,066 --> 00:03:14,662
Then the AI reads it: the client,

55
00:03:14,682 --> 00:03:17,825
the items, the address, the deadline.

56
00:03:18,077 --> 00:03:21,564
And then the rules decide. Is the address

57
00:03:21,584 --> 00:03:25,925
complete? Is the stock at the nearest warehouse?

58
00:03:26,390 --> 00:03:29,404
Which carrier is strongest on this lane?

59
00:03:29,831 --> 00:03:31,647
Can we keep the promise?

60
00:03:31,899 --> 00:03:35,559
The AI reads. The rules decide.

61
00:03:36,050 --> 00:03:39,238
And a person approves, before anything reaches

62
00:03:39,258 --> 00:03:41,022
the warehouse system.

63
00:03:41,274 --> 00:03:43,786
What if the postcode is missing?

64
00:03:44,227 --> 00:03:46,733
DeliveryLens finds the one past delivery

65
00:03:46,753 --> 00:03:49,671
site that matches, and asks the client to

66
00:03:49,691 --> 00:03:51,919
confirm it with one click.

67
00:03:52,171 --> 00:03:55,546
The client sees one address and one button.

68
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:00,548
No login. Confirmed, and the order moves on.

69
00:04:00,799 --> 00:04:04,542
An order that arrives at 08:05 misses the

70
00:04:04,562 --> 00:04:08,281
truck and waits a day. DeliveryLens adds

71
00:04:08,301 --> 00:04:12,204
a grace hour, and an urgent window until 10:00.

72
00:04:12,656 --> 00:04:18,235
That alone avoids about 729 late deliveries a year.

73
00:04:18,487 --> 00:04:21,585
At 08:00 every morning, the risk board ranks

74
00:04:21,605 --> 00:04:24,382
today's shipments by how likely they are

75
00:04:24,402 --> 00:04:27,346
to be late, and shows the fix that is still

76
00:04:27,366 --> 00:04:28,409
possible.

77
00:04:28,660 --> 00:04:32,215
We tested it on two months it had never seen.

78
00:04:32,643 --> 00:04:37,443
Of its daily top 20, 63% really were late,

79
00:04:37,463 --> 00:04:40,168
against 29% overall.

80
00:04:40,420 --> 00:04:43,840
Stock and routes shows, in three pictures,

81
00:04:43,860 --> 00:04:46,642
why some orders take the long way.

82
00:04:48,494 --> 00:04:51,503
DeliveryLens also finds the penalties Halcyon

83
00:04:51,523 --> 00:04:55,021
paid where the carrier alone was at fault:

84
00:04:55,041 --> 00:05:01,736
$261,900 in six months. The AI drafts the

85
00:05:01,756 --> 00:05:03,057
claim letter.

86
00:05:03,308 --> 00:05:06,175
For each of the 9 at-risk clients,

87
00:05:06,195 --> 00:05:08,714
DeliveryLens writes a save plan:

88
00:05:08,734 --> 00:05:12,013
talking points, an agenda, and an email,

89
00:05:12,033 --> 00:05:14,609
from that client's own deliveries.

90
00:05:14,860 --> 00:05:17,495
Every Monday, the leaders get a one-page

91
00:05:17,515 --> 00:05:21,440
brief. Anyone can ask a question in plain

92
00:05:21,460 --> 00:05:25,306
English. And every AI call is logged,

93
00:05:25,326 --> 00:05:28,263
with its cost and its guardrail checks.

94
00:05:30,115 --> 00:05:34,271
What about bad input? This email hides an

95
00:05:34,291 --> 00:05:38,105
instruction: "ignore all previous instructions."

96
00:05:38,356 --> 00:05:41,615
DeliveryLens blocks it at the safety screen.

97
00:05:42,031 --> 00:05:44,272
Nothing reaches the warehouse.

98
00:05:44,523 --> 00:05:47,757
And an unclear order is never guessed.

99
00:05:48,236 --> 00:05:50,001
It goes to a person.

100
00:05:51,852 --> 00:05:56,460
DeliveryLens is live on AWS. Every push runs

101
00:05:56,480 --> 00:06:01,408
387 backend tests and 60 frontend tests,

102
00:06:01,428 --> 00:06:03,335
then deploys itself.

103
00:06:05,186 --> 00:06:09,362
So, back to your shop. The truck arrives.

104
00:06:09,840 --> 00:06:11,804
And if it is going to be late,

105
00:06:11,824 --> 00:06:13,254
you hear first.

106
00:06:13,505 --> 00:06:19,672
From 71 to about 90 on time. About $1.4 million

107
00:06:19,692 --> 00:06:24,537
a year. Without the $680,000 tool.

108
00:06:25,588 --> 00:06:31,375
DeliveryLens. Find why. Fix it. Prove it.
